<title>USA: UN okays 40th Iraq crude contract for 900,000 bbls.</title>
<headline>UN okays 40th Iraq crude contract for 900,000 bbls.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-03-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>United Nations overseers approved the 40th Iraqi crude oil contract earlier this week for a 900,000 barrel spot sale, U.N. officials said Thursday.</p>
<p>The buyer of the contract was not immediately known,</p>
<p>Officials said there is a 41st contract pending approval, and expected further word on that contract before Friday.</p>
<p>Iraq is currently selling oil in the second 90-day period of the six-month oil-for-food deal, which started on December 10 and will end June 7.</p>
<p>Iraq is allowed to sell $2 billion worth of crude over six months, mainly to buy aid for civilians suffering under sanctions slapped on Baghdad after it invaded Kuwait in August, 1990.</p>
<p>--Patrick Connole, NY Energy Desk, +1 212 859 1828</p>
